ng" style=3D"width: 65px; border-radius: 4px;" /> Chris=E2=80=99 Corner=0D=
=0A                            </h2>=0D=0A                            <p cl=
ass=3D"corner-intro">A collection of web design and development news and th=
oughts from CodePen's own Chris Coyier.</p>=0D=0A                          =
</mj-raw>=0D=0A                          <h3>Designers</h3>=0A<p>I still th=
ink about the juicy condemnation of the design industry in Edwin Heathcote=
=E2=80=99s <a href=3D"https://post.spmailtechnol.com/f/a/O6TGC8kb6457h5thPB=
zZ8A~~/AABEfhA~/XyOj0lEV96dQKqeeSklT4gWoMs7CYex-xifmdgf1XEm1TKZUDJELGWDeJHw=
36xRHwS2MbwUrksnYJ8d5DdpbZHbAmsFX9zGpfW-CgRMWolGp-U4pioTB7qgqhaXTF7GGMBLwNN=
P8NacbVHWzOj1J8_htPX42wCqXJjuYx-mzXNG8dYcut59qv2oaR5BGh_bH">Why designers a=
bandoned their dreams of changing the world</a>.</p>=0A<blockquote><p>In th=
e heroic era of design, from the early to mid-20th century, it became a kin=
d of clich=C3=A9 to put a claim on everything. Every single thing on Earth =
not produced by nature had been designed. That was the spiel. Design wanted=
 it all.</p>=0A<p>Now Earth is a mess, its climate warming rapidly, its sea=
s full of waste. There are microplastics in the glaciers, the air is pollut=
ed and forests are being destroyed to make more stuff. If everything is des=
ign, then design is responsible for all of it.</p></blockquote>=0A<p>I thin=
k it=E2=80=99s because of my formative years as a <em>designer</em>, if I c=
an be called such a thing, there really was a high pedestal for true design=
ers. Not only is everything designed, the best design changes the world. De=
sign is the true differentiator, what makes great products what they are. W=
ith a wide umbrella as well. The entire system around a product is <em>desi=
gned</em>.</p>=0A<p>And now, perhaps, the profession has lost its shine. We=
bsites are riddled with user-hostile <em>design</em>. The term enshittifica=
tion has entered the lexicon, which is carried out at the hands of designer=
s. People aren=E2=80=99t as enamored with the products of companies that ha=
ve just laid off their buddies, sometimes at peak profitability. And I get =
it. It=E2=80=99s down times for tech outside of the you-know-what sphere. I=
=E2=80=99m down to change with the times. I don=E2=80=99t think I could hac=
k it at dentist school or whatever.</p>=0A<p>You and me, we can still do gr=
eat design. It=E2=80=99s fun and can still be rewarding. Here=E2=80=99s som=
e good advice from Sean Goedecke on software design:</p>=0A<blockquote><p>W=
hen working on large existing codebases, useful software design discussions=
 are way, way more concrete than many people believe. They typically involv=
e talking about individual files or even lines of code. You thus can=E2=80=
=99t do useful software design without being intimately familiar with the c=
odebase.</p>=0A<p><cite><a href=3D"https://post.spmailtechnol.com/f/a/7P5lp=
pSzOl4AIlgz4Na4Ag~~/AABEfhA~/tjGT_wHSesrmlXokXcu3Qou9gqhNJmk78ueNz5FGzSNER1=
PYE2bjb3NGRfwM56QG9grG1iQ8w-psa6MLNgFD75ufmOaNFf6-_fccgHuBFAfDgtn8iVSo_vA0u=
0Z57ScydFGIyGTXyDCbEuOOJrDzOwRzZAhVp_kF9xPjYDRGpPzuJSqdwpA5NdUWe_ANS5rw">Yo=
u can=E2=80=99t design software you don=E2=80=99t work on</a></cite></p>=0A=
</blockquote>=0A<p>Sean is talking about the design as code architecture, w=
hich is very legit. I think it=E2=80=99s even <em>more</em> true when it co=
mes to visual design and UX. I don=E2=80=99t think you can roll into a desi=
gn role on a product you barely know and do meaningful design on it on day =
one. You need to <em>use</em> it. Not that every decision should be make fr=
om your own personal bug-a-boos, but if you aren=E2=80=99t anywhere near fe=
eling what the users of the app are feeling when they use it, your changes =
are just stabs in the dark.</p>=0A<p>My favorite kind of web designer you=
=E2=80=99d probably call a Design Engineer. Maggie Appleton avoids describi=
ng exactly what it is (on purpose), but it has to do with understanding the=
 technological medium of which you work and implementing design with that u=
nderstanding. And, like, well. <a href=3D"https://post.spmailtechnol.com/f/=
a/xmg8D3987GTZCcDGXUR4Hw~~/AABEfhA~/j_OCQu-Z0VwN044ZuKvl9WCInURGa-lba8k8UEe=
8FVNAthRwcfs91Lym8SBE9iI7duohrWrAWTVxEQKy2uF2_Wo3mT5F42-U9akU9IkXmcPbnHkwVo=
TifpW2x6Az69LDfIcUG_quSxpzwc54t5yG8A~~">Her article pointing out examples</=
a> goes further than words alone.</p>=0A<p>One more interesting bit related=
 to all this. Sometimes design is executed by people decently far away from=
 any training and without it being a full-time job expectation. And I=E2=80=
=99m not talking about hand-scrawled signs by cash registers or graduation =
party flyers. Enter Canva. You=E2=80=99ve probably heard of it, but if not,=
 I think it=E2=80=99s fair to say it=E2=80=99s a design tool for non-design=
ers. It=E2=80=99s chock-a-block with templates, nice ones at that, and intu=
itive tools for updating those templates. Posters, social media cards, vide=
o thumbnails, logos, brochures, business cards, you name it. <em>Profession=
al</em> output for <em>professional</em> things, done in moments.</p>=0A<p>=
<a href=3D"https://post.spmailtechnol.com/f/a/dtFW92lHsxJVUD_3FmXpVw~~/AABE=
fhA~/RwZ1oBwxxn9yOqTIBORlwXBZlJuhmHXMf4YbFg0idosE20Njge3GnwYufp0smuD0711VLO=
lnQjEYNF7HgnrTQnEyZJHJq1U0E2ufdxpdj-G9jBja0YTWs3P5xUCc18NAZIALQ4PdYIe7s01qc=
SPM2lxYbrxrO5cIOJkj8R6qf7p10paITHG3EeR_HVr2mhTz">Ernie Smith puts it in an =
interesting way</a>.</p>=0A<blockquote><p>Canva makes a bold bet with Affin=
ity: The real money is in the rank-and-file office worker, not the professi=
onals. It just turned the pro tool into the loss leader.</p></blockquote>=
=0A<p>Affinity is in the league of Adobe tools like Photoshop. Canva bought=
 it and is giving it away for free, much like grocery stores put milk at th=
e back of the store. Using it means walking right past the other goods, lik=
e the easy-to-use design output tool on Aisle 2.</p>=0D=0A                 =
